"A saga orchestration diagram shows a coordinator directing local transactions and compensating actions across independently owned data.\n\nUse when: Review a distributed transaction’s successful and compensating paths. Explain which component owns workflow progress and recovery.\n\nAvoid when: One database transaction can enforce the required atomicity directly.\n\nVisual grammar: An orchestrator connects to participants through commands and outcomes. Local transaction boundaries identify independently committed changes. Compensation arrows name business reversals rather than database rollback.\n\nBuild: Choose one saga and state its business completion condition. Order local steps and record their committed effects. Define failure-triggered compensations and noncompensable steps. Draw forward flow above compensation flow with ample spacing around coordinator labels.\n\nCheck: Every compensation has a defined business meaning. Retries and duplicate outcomes preserve operation identity. Compensation failure and recovery ownership remain explicit. Opposing paths and transaction boundaries do not intersect text.\n\nPitfalls: Calling compensation an automatic rollback of all prior effects. Hiding the orchestrator’s durable progress and failure responsibilities.\n\nExample: A booking coordinator holds seats, authorizes payment, and releases the seat hold if payment is declined.\n\nAlternatives: Saga choreography diagram, Two-phase commit diagram."
